  "drafts": [
    {
      "to": "chris@relaymind.ai",
      "subject": "Re: Integrating byLLM into our product - partnership?",
      "message": "Hi Chris,\n\nThank you for reaching out. This is exciting to hear\u2014great to know that RelayMind\u2019s prototype on byLLM delivered both accuracy and cost improvements over your previous pipeline.\n\nI\u2019d be happy to discuss deeper integration and partnership opportunities, including co-marketing and a case study collaboration. I\u2019m the right person to speak with regarding partnerships and product integrations at jaseci.org.\n\nI\u2019m available for a call in the next two weeks. Please let me know what days and times work best for you, or feel free to book a time that suits your schedule via [insert calendar link if available, otherwise: a few options] such as:\n\n- Tuesday, October 15th, 10:00 AM \u2013 2:00 PM (PST)\n- Thursday, October 17th, 9:00 AM \u2013 1:00 PM (PST)\n- Monday, October 21st, 11:00 AM \u2013 3:00 PM (PST)\n\nLooking forward to exploring how we can work together.\n\nBest regards,\n\n[Your Name]\nJaseci Team\njaseci.org"
    },
    {
      "to": "fhassan@uwaterloo.ca",
      "subject": "Re: Prospective GSoC contributor - where to start?",
      "message": "Hi Fatima,\n\nIt's great to hear from you! It sounds like you're off to a great start by diving into the Jac documentation\u2014that foundational knowledge is invaluable.\n\nSince you're aiming for GSoC, you'll likely be working on the core Jaseci repository (`jaseci-org/jaseci`), but we also have several other repos worth exploring, such as `jaseci-examples` and specific plugin repositories depending on your interests (AI, blockchain, etc.).\n\nTo help you get started:\n\n1. Check out the issues labeled `good first issue` on our main GitHub repository: https://github.com/jaseci-org/jaseci/issues?q=is%3Aissue+is%3Aopen+label%3A%22good+first+issue%22\n2. Join our Discord community at https://discord.gg/jaseci to connect with the team and other contributors\u2014you can ask questions and get feedback there.\n3. Feel free to ping us with any specific areas you're interested in (e.g., CLI, SDK, plugins), and we can recommend more tailored tasks.\n\nWe love seeing proactive contributors like you, and we look forward to potentially having you on board for GSoC. Keep up the great work, and don't hesitate to reach out if you have any questions along the way!\n\nBest regards,\n\nThe Jaseci Team\nimpact@jaseci.org"
    },
    {
      "to": "omar.said@nexusanalytica.com",
      "subject": "Re: Bug: edge traversal returns duplicates after node delete (repro attached)",
      "message": "Hi Omar,\n\nThank you for the detailed report and the repro case. We are aware of this issue in Jac 0.10.2 where edge references are not immediately cleaned up from neighbor node references upon node deletion, causing duplicate results in subsequent traversals.\n\nAs a temporary workaround until the fix is shipped in the next patch release, you can:\n1. Perform a full graph sync or refresh after deletions (e.g., by re-reading the node's references from the source of truth if you maintain one).\n2. Alternatively, avoid immediate traversals right after deletions in critical paths and schedule them with a slight delay or through a scheduled agent run.\n\nWe appreciate your offer to file a GitHub issue. Please feel free to submit it with the full snippet at https://github.com/jaseci-labs/jaseci/issues so we can track it properly and provide updates when the fix is ready. You can reference this email thread for context.\n\nBest regards,\nJaSeci Team"
    },
    {
      "to": "grace.liu@techledger.news",
      "subject": "Re: Press: quote on agentic programming languages (deadline Aug 5 EOD)",
      "message": "Hi Grace,\n\nThank you for reaching out. Here is the requested quote:\n\n\"General-purpose languages treat agents as secondary abstractions, forcing developers to manually implement concurrency, state management, and communication protocols that should be fundamental. This results in fragile architectures where the boundary between an agent\u2019s internal logic and its interactions is blurred, making it difficult to reason about complex multi-agent systems. An agent-native language like Jaseci solves this by making autonomy and interaction first-class constructs, allowing engineers to focus on behavior rather than infrastructural boilerplate.\"\n\nPlease let me know if you need any further information before the deadline.\n\nBest regards,\nJaseci Team"
    },
    {
      "to": "hannah@pdxtechmeetup.org",
      "subject": "Re: August meetup - need to move your talk to Aug 19",
      "message": "Hi Hannah,\n\nNo worries at all! August 19 works perfectly for us. We'll see you then at 6:30pm.\n\nThanks for keeping us posted,\n\nBest,\nImpact Team"
    }
  ],
